'''''Gladiolus murielae''''' is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Iridaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to eastern Africa, from Ethiopia to Mozambique.<ref name="POWO">{{Cite web| title=''Gladiolus murielae'' Kelway| website=Plants of the World Online (POWO)| publisher=Kew Science| url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:437667-1| access-date=7 August 2026}}</ref> It has been given a number of English names, including '''Abyssinian gladiolus''', '''fragrant gladiolus''', '''peacock gladiolus''', and '''peacock orchid'''.<ref>{{citation |url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?kempercode=a454 |access-date=24 October 2024 |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden |title=Gardening Help: ''Gladiolus murielae''}}</ref> It was formerly placed in the genus ''Acidanthera'' as ''Acidanthera bicolor''.<ref name="POWO" />

It is a [[corm]]ous [[perennial plant|perennial]] growing to {{convert|70|-|100|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall, with linear leaves, and in late summer numerous fragrant white flowers with a maroon (occasionally orange) blotch in the throat, on slender nodding stems. Widely cultivated, it is a common subject in western and southern European gardens, where the corms are lifted every year and stored in frost-free conditions.<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref>

This plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web|title=''Gladiolus murielae'' AGM|url= https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/182096/Gladiolus-murielae/Details |work=RHS Plant Finder|publisher=Royal Horticultural Society|access-date=14 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf| title=AGM Plants - Ornamental| date=July 2017| page=43| publisher=Royal Horticultural Society| access-date=3 March 2018}}</ref>

==Pollination==
[[File:Gladiolus murielae20100819 252.jpg|thumb|left|Flower close-up]]
The long floral tube, the creamy white flowers, the marks in the throat and the sweet fragrance are typical for gladioluses that in their natural environment are [[Pollination|pollinated]] by [[Sphingidae|sphinx moths]] with a long [[proboscis]].<ref>{{Cite web| last1=Goldblatt| first1=P.| authorlink1=Peter Goldblatt| last2=Manning| first2=J.C.| authorlink2=John Charles Manning| date=1 October 2002| title=Radiation and Floral Adaptation in ''Gladiolus''| website=Pacific Horticulture| url=https://pacifichorticulture.org/articles/radiation-and-floral-adaptation-in-gladiolus/| access-date=10 August 2026}}</ref>

==Taxonomy==
The species was first described as ''Acidanthera bicolor'' by [[Christian Ferdinand Friedrich Hochstetter|Christian Hochstetter]] in 1844.<ref>{{Cite journal| author=Hochstetter, C.F.| date=1844| title=Nova genera plantarum Africae| journal=Flora oder botanische Zeitung| volume=27| issue=2| pages=25| url=http://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/39139}}</ref> In 1973, [[Wessel Marais]] included the genus ''Acidanthera'' in ''Gladiolus''. As the name ''Gladiolus bicolor'' had already been published by [[Carl Peter Thunberg]] in 1784,<ref>{{Cite book| author=Thunberg, C.P.| date=1784| title=D.D. Gladiolus| page=16| url=http://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/2953220}}</ref> for a taxon now considered to be the same as ''[[Sparaxis villosa]]'',<ref>{{Cite web| title=''Gladiolus bicolor'' Thunb.| website=Plants of the World Online (POWO)| publisher=Kew Science| url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:437293-1| access-date=7 August 2026}}</ref> and in 1877 again by [[John Gilbert Baker]],<ref>{{Cite journal| author=Baker, J.G.| date=1877| title=Systema Iridacearum| journal=Journal of the Linnean Society, Botany, London| volume=16| issue=91| page=178| url=http://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/223459}}</ref> as a [[nomen novum]] for ''Gladiolus luteus'' {{small|[[Friedrich Wilhelm Klatt|Klatt]]}} non {{small|[[Jean-Baptiste Lamarck|Lam.]]}}, a taxon now considered to be the same as ''[[Gladiolus virescens]]'',<ref>{{Cite web| title=''Gladiolus bicolor'' Baker| website=Plants of the World Online (POWO)| publisher=Kew Science| url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:437294-1| access-date=7 August 2026}}</ref> Marais needed another name. He chose to call the species ''Gladiolus callianthus''. Marais was apparently unaware that nurseryman [[James Kelway]] had already validly published the name ''Gladiolus murielae'' in 1932, so that the name ''G. callianthus'' was superfluous. Kelway published the name in [[The Gardeners' Chronicle]], a horticultural magazine mostly overlooked by botanists. The epithet ''murielae'' honours Muriel Erskine; her husband E.N. Erskine had collected the specimen in Ethiopia on which Kelway based the name.<ref>{{Cite web| title=''Gladiolus murielae'' Kelway, Gard. Chron. ser. 3, 92(2381): 107 (1932)| website=International Plant Names Index (IPNI)| publisher=Kew Science| url=https://www.ipni.org/n/437667-1| access-date=7 August 2026}}</ref>

The species is often still offered for sale under the name ''Acidanthera bicolor'', sometimes with ''murielae'' added as an [[infraspecific name (botany)|infraspecific name]],<ref name=LaCr12>{{Cite journal| last=La Croix| first=Isobyl| year=2012| title=A selection of tropical ''Gladiolus''| journal=The Plantsman| series=New Series| volume=11| issue=3| pages=184–187}}</ref> or even as the [[cultivar]] name 'Murielae'.<ref name=Evans>{{citation |url=http://www.ces.ncsu.edu/depts/hort/consumer/factsheets/bulbs-summer/acida_bi.html |title=Acidanthera bicolor, Gladiolus callianthus; Abyssinian, Fragrant gladiolus, Sword lily |access-date=2007-06-29 |last=Evans |first=Erv |publisher=[[NC State University]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070904043142/http://www.ces.ncsu.edu/depts/hort/consumer/factsheets/bulbs-summer/acida_bi.html |archive-date=2007-09-04 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

